[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How does a user express that they care about a task? → Declared watch, extensible matcher (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: which changes are worth a notification. This is where a watch list turns into either a useful signal or noise
  you turn off.

  [1mThe fork:[0m notify on [3mlifecycle[0m changes (things that alter what you'd do about the task) versus [3many[0m mutation (including
  someone fixing a typo in the title). Noise is the failure mode that kills notification systems, and it's cheap to
  widen the set later, expensive to win back trust after people mute everything.

  - [1mLifecycle only[0m — status/completion, due date, assignee, deletion. High signal, small event vocabulary, less to test.
    Misses edits that sometimes do matter (a rewritten description that changes the task).
  - [1mEvery change[0m — any field diff produces an event. Complete, no "why didn't I get told," but title/description churn
    dominates the feed and each event type still needs its own rendering.
  - [1mPer-subscription preferences[0m — you choose, per watched task, what to hear about. Most control, but it's a settings
    surface before we know what anyone actually wants muted, and it needs UI on every watch toggle.

  [1mMy recommendation: lifecycle only[0m, with the event type as an open string in the payload so widening the set is adding
  an emitter, not changing the schema. Preferences can come later as a filter between matcher and delivery — it slots
  into the same seam.

